{
  "notes": "No ordering, atomic switch, or queue drain is required. The customer change is backward-compatible: offer.queued keeps the existing {customerId, offerId} shape. New customer instances stop creating offers for implied consent, while old customer instances may still create those events; both old and new notifier versions can consume the unchanged event. A rollback to the old customer version is safe, including while the notifier daily-cap version is deployed."
}
# Customer release

## Rollout order

Deploy customer independently, one instance at a time; no dependency on notifier deployment order is required. The default pipeline order is therefore acceptable. Do not require an atomic switch or a queue drain.

## Mixed-version behavior

Old customer instances continue to treat `implied` consent as marketing-allowed and may publish the existing `offer.queued {customerId, offerId}` event. New customer instances treat only `explicit` consent as allowed and publish the same event shape. Old and new notifier instances can consume either event shape, so traffic can be served by mixed customer versions and queued events can remain in the database during rollout.

## Rollback

If rollout fails, stop and route traffic back to the old customer instances, or redeploy the old customer version one instance at a time. No schema migration or event transformation is needed. Any already queued events remain processable; the notifier's daily-cap behavior may still apply to them. After rollback, old customer instances may again queue offers for implied-consent customers, which is compatible with both notifier versions.

